 -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "511398e8-780e-57b2-8f1b-092e9f6cca45" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Scalar Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae97dcf2-9fa2-5790-a789-bb9c6d85c180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173881} {:answer/id #uuid "e168ae21-e23c-52ca-aaa3-cb9b55b51f74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173882} {:answer/id #uuid "e596389a-7199-5d58-9ddf-04b36a33795e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173883} {:answer/id #uuid "6223a1e5-655c-58e9-a471-0be031ca2519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173884} {:answer/id #uuid "630640de-9b44-58f4-8e25-0e4846352175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae97dcf2-9fa2-5790-a789-bb9c6d85c180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173881} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d2bdeed-ef33-5ca7-99f5-44a7957a7472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f458812-6145-5d6d-87ea-ffca6f06ff2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-112448" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=-5I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c21b78a8-2fa7-5746-a5be-bbd5eb9b35c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380674} {:answer/id #uuid "80e79a9d-a2f9-58ab-a2bf-3af3172c71d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380675} {:answer/id #uuid "605d34ec-e855-54ce-a2fc-8011c0a1a54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380676} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d225bd-1e31-54d7-9fb8-69a5096f30be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380677} {:answer/id #uuid "3447641e-8656-501b-8ab8-d20cec284b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c21b78a8-2fa7-5746-a5be-bbd5eb9b35c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380674} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba42c2a0-015b-5e3d-b56e-c1d499e98dbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86ca96fe-ce68-51c6-b6c2-48af3e332f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112475" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot (4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
